    "final_text": "I've built the todo CLI with all four commands. The full test suite passes (96 tests, 0 failures), ESLint reports no errors, and the app has no runtime dependencies.\n\n**What's there**\n- `index.js`: the entry point. It reads the command, prints results to stdout and errors to stderr, and exits with 0 on success or 1 on any error.\n- `src/todos.js`: the todo logic (add, complete, delete, list formatting, ID parsing), kept separate from file access so it can be tested on its own.\n- `src/store.js`: reads, checks and saves `./todos.json`.\n- `test/`: tests for the logic, for saving and loading real files, and end-to-end tests that run `node index.js` in a fresh temp folder.\n- `package.json`: `npm test` uses Node's built-in test runner; `npm run lint` runs ESLint (the only dev dependency).\n- `README.md`: usage and the file format.\n\n**Decisions I made where the spec was open**\n- **The file is `{ \"nextId\": N, \"todos\": [...] }`, not a bare list.** Storing the next ID means a deleted ID is never handed out again. With a bare list and \"highest ID + 1\", deleting the newest todo would recycle its number, so `complete 3` could later hit a different todo.\n- **A damaged `todos.json` is never overwritten.** If it isn't valid JSON or has the wrong shape, every command stops with an error naming the problem, and the file is left untouched. A missing file just means an empty list, and `list` doesn't create one.\n- **Saves are crash-safe.** The app writes a temp file and then renames it over `todos.json`, so an interrupted save can't leave a half-written file.\n- **Input rules:**\n  - IDs must be plain digits and at least 1, so `abc`, `0`, `-1`, `1.5` and `1e3` are rejected.\n  - `complete` and `delete` take exactly one ID.\n  - `add Buy groceries` works without quotes.\n  - Text is trimmed and runs of whitespace, including line breaks, become one space so each todo stays on one line.\n  - Empty text is rejected.\n- **Completing a todo that's already done** prints a note and still exits 0.\n- **Extras:** `help`, `--help` and `-h` print usage. Running with no command or an unknown one prints usage and exits 1.\n\n**Verification**\n- The tests cover success and error cases for every command, including missing arguments, IDs that don't exist, an empty list, a damaged file (checked for each command), and emoji and non-Latin text.\n- To check the tests actually catch bugs, I broke the code in seven places (ID counting, the already-done check, ID parsing, delete, temp-file cleanup, duplicate-ID detection, extra-argument rejection). Each break made at least one test fail.\n- A second run with a private temp folder left no files behind.\n\n**Other notes**\n- The repo's commit hook required a different commit style and a Dex co-author line instead of the Claude one, so the commits on `feat/cli-todo-app` use that. There's no remote, so nothing is pushed. I left your `CLAUDE.md` uncommitted.\n- I skipped two guidelines on purpose. File locking was left out, so two changes made at the same instant can lose one; the README says so. The API and rate-limiter guidance doesn't apply to a CLI.\n\n**Possible follow-ups (not built):** file locking for simultaneous use, an `edit` or `undo` command, and a setting to store `todos.json` somewhere other than the current folder.",
